t(id="OP-4", slug="take-verify-main-snapshot", title="Take and verify a fresh main-corpus snapshot",
  s=["S-01"], wave=1, area=["ops"], tier="Haiku", size="S",
  why="The newest main snapshot is from 2026-07-30 (manifest records 11,026 papers; the live database has 12,390) and every backup sits on the same device as the live data, which is 98% full with 91 GB free (review item g, `df -h`). The next write, re-embed or bad migration on that device has no recent restore point. This is the only finding whose downside is unrecoverable.",
  in_=["Run the existing `python -m app.snapshot` for the main corpus from its data directory, once.","Verify the new snapshot read-only: `PRAGMA quick_check` on the snapshot copy opened with `mode=ro`, and `count(*)` of papers and chunks equal to the manifest. Record sizes, timings and `df` before/after in a dated record under `docs/eval-reports/data/`."],
  out=["Deleting any old snapshot, `.bak` or `.partial` file, and copying to another device (operator question F1; no target exists).","Any code change (OP-3 adds the checks to the tool). Any write to `papers.db`."],
  owns=["docs/eval-reports/data/<run-date>-main-snapshot-record/"],
  commits=["stub: record file with the four acceptance checks as an unchecked list and the abort rule (stop if under 20 GB free)","snapshot taken; manifest copied into the record","verification results: quick_check, counts vs manifest, age","free-space and next-step notes (off-device copy is F1)"],
  verify=["`sqlite3 \"file:<snapshot-db>?mode=ro\" \"PRAGMA quick_check\"` prints `ok`","`sqlite3 \"file:<snapshot-db>?mode=ro\" \"select count(*) from papers\"` equals the manifest `papers`","`df -h /` before and after is quoted"],
  accept="Newest main snapshot is under 24 hours old at verification time, quick_check is ok, restored paper count equals the manifest, and free space after is still at least 20 GB. Falsified if any of the four fails; then the snapshot is deleted and the failure reported (nothing else is touched).",
  deps=[], ext=["F1"], gates=["EV-14","CQ-7"],
  needs_note="operator go-ahead to write about 9 GB (estimate, RV-6) into the main data dir's backups folder on a 98%-full device",
  risk="Disk fills mid-write on a 98%-full device. Mitigation: abort rule before starting; the tool reads the source read-only and writes only the new snapshot. Rollback: delete the new snapshot directory.",
  docs="BACKLOG and PROJECT-STATUS rows via EP-3; the restore procedure itself is documented by EP-7."),

t(id="OP-3", slug="snapshot-integrity-and-checksums", title="Add integrity check and checksums to snapshot manifests",
  s=["S-01"], wave=1, area=["ops"], tier="Sonnet", size="S",
  why="`app/snapshot.py::backup_sqlite` is correct (read-only source plus VACUUM INTO) but never runs an integrity check and the manifest records no checksum per artifact (RV-6 F-A1, F-A7; review S-01), so a corrupt snapshot looks identical to a good one until the day it is restored.",
  in_=["Run `PRAGMA quick_check` on the produced SQLite copy and fail the snapshot (non-zero exit, snapshot marked incomplete) if it is not `ok`.","Write sha256 per artifact into the manifest; add a verify mode (or reuse an existing one) that recomputes them and compares `count(*)` with the manifest."],
  out=["The guard against a nonexistent data dir (OP-6). Taking a snapshot (OP-4). Off-device copy (F1)."],
  owns=["app/snapshot.py","app/test_snapshot.py"],
  commits=["stub: docstring stating the manifest fields (`quick_check`, `sha256` per artifact) and the failure rule","failing tests: corrupt copy fails; manifest has sha256; verify detects a flipped byte","implementation to green","docstring and `--help` text brought up to date"],
  verify=["`conda run -n agent-rag-research python -m pytest -q app/test_snapshot.py -o addopts=\"--disable-socket\"`","Mutation proof (CONVENTIONS section 14): revert the quick_check call and watch the corruption test fail"],
  accept="A deliberately corrupted SQLite copy makes the snapshot exit non-zero and not write a complete manifest; a flipped byte in an artifact fails verify; existing snapshot tests stay green. Falsified if either test passes with the check removed.",
  deps=[], gates=["OP-6","EP-7"],
  risk="A verify mode changes the manifest shape: old manifests lack checksums. Mitigation: verify treats a missing checksum as 'unverifiable', not 'ok'. Rollback: revert.",
  docs="PROJECT-STATUS section 2 entry-point row for any new flag (via EP-3); RUNBOOK via EP-7."),

t(id="OP-6", slug="wrong-corpus-guards", title="Guard every corpus tool against a missing or mismatched database",
  s=["S-07"], wave=1, area=["ops"], tier="Sonnet", size="M",
  why="Only `app.serve --data-dir` refuses a nonexistent database (`app/serve.py:80-82` has no exists check on the default branch). `app/corpus_integrity.py` opens the live database read-write, and the collection-versus-database pairing is checked nowhere, so a mispointed tool returns a confident empty or wrong corpus (the T-DOC56 shape; RV-6 F-A4, RV-3 F-1.2; review S-07). `app/dashboard/status.py:830` falls back to the literal collection name `papers`.",
  in_=["One shared `require_existing_db()` (search for an existing helper first, e.g. the T-DOC56 `_resolve_store_paths`; add a module only if none fits) used by `corpus_integrity` (also opened `mode=ro`), `snapshot`, `reindex_idf`.","`app.doctor`: collection point count must match the database within 1% (stated tolerance), reported with both numbers.","Remove the `papers` fallback at `app/dashboard/status.py:830`."],
  out=["The two `app/serve.py` branches (folded into OP-2, which owns that file's startup). Changing `migrate()` (protected).","Auto-snapshot before migration (RV-6 F-A7, not carried by the review)."],
  owns=["app/corpus_paths.py","app/test_corpus_paths.py","app/corpus_integrity.py","app/test_corpus_integrity.py","app/snapshot.py","app/test_snapshot.py","app/reindex_idf.py","app/test_reindex_idf.py","app/doctor.py","app/test_doctor.py","app/dashboard/status.py","app/dashboard/test_status.py"],
  commits=["stub: docstring of `require_existing_db()` stating precondition and error message; list of the five call sites","failing tests per call site (nonexistent path exits non-zero, names the path)","helper + call sites to green; `corpus_integrity` opens `mode=ro` (test asserts a write attempt fails)","doctor point-count-vs-database check with injected counts; status fallback removed"],
  verify=["`conda run -n agent-rag-research python -m app.corpus_integrity --help` still works; each tool against a nonexistent data dir exits non-zero (shown in the PR)","`conda run -n agent-rag-research python -m app.doctor --cpu-only` from each data dir still passes where services are up (needs services: run only when the vector store is up)"],
  accept="Every named tool exits non-zero against a nonexistent path with a message that names the path; `corpus_integrity` cannot write; doctor run from each of the five data dirs passes its pairing check on the current corpora. Falsified if any tool still prints a result for a nonexistent path or if the doctor check fails on a correctly paired corpus.",
  deps=["OP-3"], gates=["OP-5","OP-2"],
  services="vector store (CPU container) only for the doctor smoke run; unit tests use fakes",
  risk="A false positive in the 1% tolerance blocks a healthy doctor run. Mitigation: tolerance is a named constant, the message prints both counts. Rollback: revert; the guards are additive.",
  docs="PROJECT-STATUS section 2 (doctor behaviour) via EP-3; RUNBOOK via EP-7."),

t(id="OP-5", slug="gpu-lock-holder-and-fast-fail", title="Name the GPU lock holder and fail fast on the query path",
  s=["S-06"], wave=1, area=["ops"], tier="Sonnet", size="M",
  why="A query waits up to 300 s for the GPU lock before erroring (`app/assembly.py:630-640` passes no `gpu_lock_timeout`, default 300 s in the embedder and reranker), the timeout message does not name the holder (`rag/gpu_lock.py:38-41`), and doctor reports only 'unreachable' when a foreign process holds the GPU (OP-1 handles 'TEI down', not 'GPU held'; RV-1 F-1.9, RV-6 F-A3 and F-A8; review S-06). A foreign model server holds 18,609 of 24,576 MiB today.",
  in_=["`FileGpuLock` writes pid, stage and UTC time to a `<lock>.holder` file on acquire (best effort, removed on release) and the timeout error reads it back.","Query path only: pass a short `gpu_lock_timeout` (proposed 20 s, a named constant; ingest keeps 300 s) with an actionable message.","`app.doctor`: list foreign GPU compute processes via `nvidia-smi --query-compute-apps` and read the TEI container exit code and OOM log line, so 'GPU held by a foreign process' is distinct from 'TEI crashed' (nvidia-smi and docker calls injected so tests need neither)."],
  out=["Marking eval runs invalid on lock timeouts (EV-10). A priority scheduler for the lock (not needed; the holder file is the smallest step). Reranker-failure fallback to fused order (changes behaviour, needs a Coverage flag: foundation).","The Config field for the timeout (RIDER-1 item 1)."],
  owns=["rag/gpu_lock.py","rag/test_gpu_lock.py","app/doctor.py","app/test_doctor.py","app/assembly.py","app/test_assembly.py"],
  commits=["stub: docstrings stating the holder-file format and the 20 s query-path timeout","failing tests: second lock with timeout 0.1 raises with the first's stage label; query-path construction passes the short timeout; doctor names a foreign pid from a canned nvidia-smi output","implementation to green","doctor TEI exit-code and OOM-line reading with injected docker output"],
  verify=["Two `FileGpuLock` objects on one path in a unit test (no GPU)","Live check only when the operator frees the GPU: hold the lock from a second process, `semantic_search` returns the named error in under 22 s"],
  accept="Holding the lock from a second process makes the query path fail in under 22 s with the holder named; doctor output against a canned foreign-holder state names the pid and says 'GPU held by a foreign process', not 'TEI unreachable'. Falsified if the error still waits the full default or omits the holder.",
  deps=["OP-6"], gates=["OP-2","EV-10","EP-7","RIDER-1"],
  signoff="n for the code; the 20 s value is a decision in PR review. Moving it into Config rides RIDER-1 item 1.",
  risk="A 20 s ceiling will fail queries that today would have waited out a long GPU job. That is the intended behaviour but changes what an agent sees. Mitigation: ingest is unaffected; the value is one constant. Rollback: revert.",
  docs="PROJECT-STATUS section 2 (doctor flags) via EP-3; RUNBOOK GPU-contention section via EP-7."),

